From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:32:27 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 100/119] fix: the wave glare's source was a DENOISED copy of
 the frame, and denoising is what a glare source cannot survive

TEST-CHECKLIST F3: at a NIGHT exposure the FFT rays need Streak Amount 110
(+6 stops) to be barely visible, where round 2 predicted they would be obvious
at 50. Traced the whole path numerically for a 200 kcd/m2 practical at EV 2
(pre-exposure 2083, exposed source 41 667 = 17.8 stops over mid grey).

Everything downstream is clean: the threshold's soft knee has no clamp; the
5-tap source blur's weights sum to exactly 1 and its fp16 peak is 16 790,
nowhere near overflow; the sprite deposit is a float and carries no DOF-style
energy-split clamp; the kernel's Parseval normalisation, its 1/u^2 ring and its
sqrt/k^2 encode are pure geometry and never see a scene unit; the composite has
no bloom weight left and the ODT puts mid grey at mid grey.

The loss is entirely in the first stage. $HDRTarget 1/2a is built by
CStableDownsamplePass with firefly suppression on (r_HDRBloomQuality defaults to
2), and firefly suppression exists to pull a bright OUTLIER down towards its
block's mean - which is the definition of a glare source. For a lamp covering
one of a block's four taps: 0.3466 out where the true mean is 10 417, a factor
of 30 000, 14.9 stops. And 0.3466 is BELOW the threshold (0.18*2^4 = 2.88), so
at night a small lamp was never extracted at all and no Streak Amount could
bring it back. A large lamp round-trips exactly, which is why the report is
"barely visible" and not "never".

Per ray pixel, using the spec's own 0.5 % figure: physical is 0.42, a stop over
mid grey and obvious; through the old chain it was 1.4e-5, 13.6 stops under
grey, and +6 stops of gain still left it 7.6 stops under. Round 2's physics was
right; the path was eating fifteen stops.

Two fixes. The knee moved to the camera's white in item 2 of this round, which
takes the 1-of-4 case from x30 000 to x11.6. The residual 3.5 stops is the
filter doing its job, and the glare extraction is the one consumer that must not
have it done: under the switch the wave glare's threshold pass now reads the
FULL-RESOLUTION $HDRTarget. The bilinear read at the half-res destination is an
exact 2x2 box, so it is the same output resolution at one texture read's extra
bandwidth. $HDRTarget 1/2a still sets the sprite grid.

Deliberately asymmetric: the analytic streaks, the anamorphic flare, halation
and the front filter keep the half-res copy, because their amounts are creative
sliders tuned against it and the wave path is the only one whose numbers are
claimed to be physical. The threshold is still shared. The
r_HDRDiffractionDebug log line now names which source was read.

decisions/s8-glare-source.md has the full trace and the three approaches not
taken (kill the suppression outright; a separate unfiltered downsample for the
whole glare family; raise the default again). DiffractionKernelSpec.md 2.4 is
corrected: "a game light's HDR value is clamped to a few tens" - the sentence
that produced the 20 and then 50 default - is no longer true under the switch.

