+> **Corrected 2026-08-29 (scene-referred round 3).** The sentence above — "a game light's HDR
+> value is clamped to a few tens" — is the reason `Streak Amount`'s default was 20 and then 50,
+> and on the scene-referred path it is **no longer true**. A 200 kcd/m² practical at a night
+> exposure arrives in the HDR target at 41 667, seventeen and a half stops over mid grey, and its
+> rays at 0.5 % of the source land about a stop OVER mid grey. The physics deposits plenty.
+>
+> Two things had been eating it, both fixed in round 3 and both documented in
+> `scene-notes/decisions/s8-glare-source.md`:
+>
+> 1. The half-resolution source `$HDRTarget 1/2a` is produced with firefly suppression on
+>    (`r_HDRBloomQuality` 2), and firefly suppression exists to pull bright OUTLIERS down towards
+>    their block's mean — which is exactly what a glare source is. On a lamp a few pixels across
+>    that cost a factor of 30 000, and took the source below the threshold entirely, so no
+>    `Streak Amount` could recover it. Under the switch the wave glare's threshold pass now reads
+>    the FULL-RESOLUTION `$HDRTarget` (the bilinear read at the half-res destination is an exact
+>    2×2 box); `$HDRTarget 1/2a` still sets the sprite grid. The rest of the glare family keeps
+>    the half-res copy — see the decision note for why that asymmetry is deliberate.
+> 2. That filter's compression knee sat at a literal 1.0, display white. It moves to the camera's
+>    white, 14 stops over mid grey (`decisions/s8-fp16-writers.md`).
+>
+> `Streak Amount` keeps its round-2 meaning — gain in stops, ten units per stop, 50 = physical
+> (`decisions/s7-glare-gain.md`) — and 50 is now visible on a night practical, which is what it
+> was always supposed to be. A DAYLIGHT-exposed 200 kcd/m² lamp is still faint at 50 and still
+> wants 130–150; that is physics, not a defect.
+
 `Streak Amount` is therefore defined as a **source radiance boost**: how much brighter the real
 source is than the clamped HDR value standing in for it. `1` means "the HDR value IS the true
 radiance" — physically exact, nearly invisible. `10–100` reproduces the star an actual lamp or
@@ -234,6 +259,13 @@ sun of that kind would draw. Nothing about the pattern's **shape or falloff** ch
